Make the Polychrome USB colour order follow the RGSwap register.

The ASRock Polychrome USB controller can swap the red and green bytes of
every colour it is given, per zone, through the RGSwap register. The
driver reads the power-on value of that register at init and stores it in
device_info.rgswap, then uses it to decide the byte order it packs.

That is correct until the mode changes. Entering Direct calls
SetRGSwap(false), which writes an all-zero register -- upstream does this
because rewriting the register makes the headers flash. Leaving Direct
calls SetRGSwap(true), which writes the stored value back. Neither
updates device_info.rgswap, so in Direct the driver keeps compensating in
software for a swap it has just switched off in hardware, and red and
green come out exchanged.

Measured here on a B650I Lightning WiFi (26CE:01A2), by talking to
/dev/hidraw0 directly: the board reports RGSwap 0x0c, so both addressable
headers swap. Writing 0x00 to the register and then sending R=0xff to a
header lights it green; writing 0x0c back and sending the same byte
lights it red.

So remember what was last written and pack against that, rather than
against a value the device no longer holds. This is the reported
behaviour in OpenRGB issue #2894.

Not sent upstream yet: the fix is right but untestable where it matters
most, because per-LED streaming produces nothing at all on this board --
see the openrgb entry in configuration.nix.

--- a/Controllers/ASRockPolychromeUSBController/ASRockPolychromeUSBController.h
+++ b/Controllers/ASRockPolychromeUSBController/ASRockPolychromeUSBController.h
@@ -142,6 +142,7 @@
     hid_device*                         dev;
     std::vector<PolychromeDeviceInfo>   device_info;
     std::string                         location;
+    bool                                rgswaplive[8] = { 0 };
 
     void WriteRGSwap
         (
--- a/Controllers/ASRockPolychromeUSBController/ASRockPolychromeUSBController.cpp
+++ b/Controllers/ASRockPolychromeUSBController/ASRockPolychromeUSBController.cpp
@@ -237,7 +237,7 @@
     usb_buf[0x03] = device_info.zone_type;
 	usb_buf[0x04] = mode;
 
-    if(device_info.rgswap)
+    if(rgswaplive[device_info.zone_type])
     {
         usb_buf[0x05] = RGBGetRValue(rgb);
         usb_buf[0x06] = RGBGetGValue(rgb);
@@ -273,7 +273,7 @@
 
         for(std::size_t led_idx = 0; led_idx < zones[zone_idx].leds_count; led_idx++)
         {
-            if(device_info.rgswap)
+            if(rgswaplive[device_info.zone_type])
             {
                 combined_leds_rgb.push_back(RGBGetRValue(zones[zone_idx].colors[led_idx]));
                 combined_leds_rgb.push_back(RGBGetGValue(zones[zone_idx].colors[led_idx]));
@@ -382,6 +382,16 @@
     )
 {
     unsigned char rgconfig[1] = {static_cast<unsigned char>((((unsigned char)chnl8 << 7) | ((unsigned char)pcb << 6) | ((unsigned char)io << 5) | ((unsigned char)pch << 4) | ((unsigned char)ahdr1 << 3) | ((unsigned char)ahdr0 << 2) | ((unsigned char)hdr1 << 1) | (unsigned char)hdr0))};
+
+    /*-----------------------------------------------------------------*\
+    | Remember what the device was actually told, so that the colour    |
+    | order below follows the register instead of the power-on value    |
+    \*-----------------------------------------------------------------*/
+    rgswaplive[0] = hdr0;  rgswaplive[1] = hdr1;
+    rgswaplive[2] = ahdr0; rgswaplive[3] = ahdr1;
+    rgswaplive[4] = pch;   rgswaplive[5] = io;
+    rgswaplive[6] = pcb;   rgswaplive[7] = chnl8;
+
     WriteHeader(POLYCHROME_USB_RGSWAP_CFG, rgconfig, 1);
 }
 
